.\" UNIX V7 source code: see /COPYRIGHT or www.tuhs.org for details.
.TH DF 1M
.SH NAME
df \- disk free
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B df
[ filesystem ] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Df
prints out the number of free blocks
available on the
.I filesystems.
If no file system is specified,
the free space on all of
the normally mounted file systems
is printed.
.PP
.B "Note:"
a
.I filesystem
here is the name of a
.IR device ,
not of a mount point: there is no
.IR /etc/mtab ,
.IR mount (1M)
not being ported.
The count is obtained by walking the free list itself, block by block, exactly as the
kernel's
.IR alloc ()
drains it.
The superblock's
.I s_tfree
field is
.B not
consulted and must not be: it is dead in this port and nothing maintains it.
.SH "BLOCKS ARE 1024 BYTES"
.B "Note:"
the block
.I df
counts in is
.B 1024
bytes.
It is not this machine's filesystem block, which is
.B 3072
bytes \- 512 words of 48 bits \- and is three of them.
.PP
Two things follow.
Every number
.I df
prints is a
.B multiple of three,
the smallest thing that can be allocated being one 3072-byte block: a one-byte file
costs three.
And the numbers are
.B half
what a PDP\-11 printed, v7 having counted in 512-byte blocks \- not a sixth, which is what
they were when this program counted in the filesystem block.
.PP
.B "Note:"
there is no
.I BSHIFT
on this machine and there cannot be, 3072 not being a power of two; the conversion is a
divide by the filesystem block and then a multiply, and both live in
.IR sys/param.h .
.PP
.IR Du (1),
.IR quot (1M)
and
.IR ls (1)
report in the same block.
.SH "SUPER-USER ONLY"
.B "Note:"
.I df
reads the raw disk, and
.I /dev/rmd0
is mode 0600 because that one node is the contents of every file on the volume.
So this program works for the super-user and for nobody else.
It is deliberately
.B not
set-user-id: a set-uid
.I df
would hand the whole filesystem to anyone able to think of an offset.
.SH FILES
.I /dev/rmd0
\- the raw disk, and the default when no
.I filesystem
is named.
There is one EC\-5052 drive of 2000 blocks and there are no partitions; swap is on the
drums.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B "cannot open"
names a device that could not be read;
.I df
goes on to the next argument and exits 0 either way, which is v7's behaviour and is kept.
.PP
.B "bad free count"
and
.B "bad free block"
mean the free list is corrupt at the block named, and the number printed up to that point
is a lower bound rather than an answer.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
du(1), quot(1M), ls(1)
.br
.B "Note:"
v7's SEE ALSO named
.IR icheck (1M),
which is not ported.
